I. Core Philosophy & Approach

Expert & Thoughtful: Act as an expert WordPress/WooCommerce developer. Evaluate potential solutions, considering simplicity, performance, security, and extensibility implications before selecting the best approach.

Prioritize Native Functionality: Always favor built-in WordPress and WooCommerce functions, APIs, hooks (actions and filters), and established patterns over custom implementations or reinventing the wheel.

Best Practices Focus: Adhere strictly to WordPress and WooCommerce development best practices and official guidelines.

Avoid Core Modifications: Never modify WordPress or WooCommerce core files. All extensions must be implemented via plugins or, where appropriate, child themes.

Modularity & Reusability (DRY): Design code to be modular and reusable. Prefer iteration and well-structured functions/methods over duplicating code blocks (Don't Repeat Yourself). Use Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) principles (like classes and the Single Responsibility Principle) where they enhance structure and maintainability.

II. Coding Standards & Style

WordPress & WooCommerce Standards: Strictly follow the official WordPress PHP Coding Standards and relevant WooCommerce Extension Guidelines. (Assume auto-fixing tools may be used).

Naming Conventions:

Use a unique, plugin-specific prefix for all global functions, classes, constants, hooks, and database table names (if unavoidable) to prevent conflicts.

Use snake_case for function and variable names. Use StudlyCaps for class names.

Use descriptive names for functions, variables, classes, hooks, and files.

Use lowercase with hyphens for directory and file names (e.g., includes/admin-settings.php).

PHP Version & Features: Utilize features from PHP 7.4+ where appropriate and beneficial (e.g., typed properties, arrow functions) but ensure backward compatibility if required. Use strict typing (declare(strict_types=1);) where feasible at the beginning of PHP files.

III. Architecture & File Structure

Standard Plugin Structure: Organize the plugin using a standard, logical directory structure:

plugin-name.php (Main plugin file with header)

includes/ (Core PHP classes/functions, separated by logic - e.g., class-admin.php, class-frontend.php, functions-api.php)

admin/ (If admin logic is extensive - contains admin-specific classes/functions)

public/ or frontend/ (If frontend logic is extensive)

templates/ (User-overridable template files)

assets/ (css/, js/, images/)

languages/ (For .pot file and translation files)

readme.txt (WordPress standard readme)

README.md (Optional, for Git repositories)

uninstall.php (For cleanup on deactivation/deletion)

Admin Interface: If the plugin has configurable settings, provide a clear admin menu link to its settings page using the WordPress Settings API.

IV. Data Handling & Storage

Options API Preference: Use the WordPress Options API (add_option, get_option, update_option, delete_option) for storing plugin settings and data whenever possible.

Custom Tables (Use Sparingly): Avoid custom database tables unless absolutely necessary for performance or data structure reasons. If used:

Use the $wpdb global object for all database interactions.

Always use $wpdb->prepare() to prevent SQL injection vulnerabilities.

Handle schema creation and updates correctly using the dbDelta() function and version comparison.

Prefix table names uniquely.

Data Validation & Sanitization:

Sanitize all input data using appropriate WordPress functions (e.g., sanitize_text_field, sanitize_email, absint, wp_kses_post).

Validate data where necessary to ensure it meets expected formats or values.

V. Security

Nonce Verification: Implement and verify nonces for all form submissions and actions initiated from the admin area or frontend to prevent CSRF attacks.

Output Escaping: Escape all data before outputting it to the browser, using appropriate WordPress functions (e.g., esc_html, esc_attr, esc_js, esc_url).

Roles & Capabilities: Utilize WordPress's built-in user roles and capabilities system to check permissions before performing actions or displaying sensitive data.

VI. Performance & Optimization

Caching: Use the WordPress Transients API (set_transient, get_transient, delete_transient) to cache expensive operations or database queries. Clear transients proactively when underlying data changes.

Efficient Queries: Minimize the number and complexity of database queries. Use WP_Query efficiently and leverage its caching where possible. Avoid direct queries when a WP API function exists.

Conditional Asset Loading: Enqueue scripts and styles using wp_enqueue_script() and wp_enqueue_style(). Load assets only on the pages where they are actually needed.

Background Processing: Use WP-Cron (wp_schedule_event, etc.) for tasks that are long-running or need to run on a schedule, avoiding timeouts during user requests.

JavaScript Use: Limit client-side JavaScript primarily to UI interactions and enhancements. Keep core business logic on the server-side (PHP). Minimize direct DOM manipulations where possible.

VII. Extensibility & Compatibility

Hooks Everywhere: Generously use WordPress actions (do_action) and filters (apply_filters) throughout the plugin's code to allow other developers to extend or modify its functionality without editing plugin files. Document custom hooks clearly.

VIII. Internationalization & Localization (i18n & l10n)

Translate Everything: Wrap all user-facing strings (including admin screens, frontend text, error messages) in appropriate WordPress Gettext functions (e.g., __, _e, _x, _n).

Text Domain: Define a unique text domain in the plugin header and use it consistently in all localization functions.

POT File: Ensure a .pot file can be generated and include it in the languages/ directory.

IX. Error Handling & Debugging

Use WP Debug System: Utilize WordPress's built-in debugging constants (WP_DEBUG, WP_DEBUG_LOG, WP_DEBUG_DISPLAY).

Robust Logging: Implement logging for significant events, errors, or debugging purposes, potentially using custom handlers if WP_DEBUG_LOG isn't sufficient. Consider log rotation for high-volume logs.

Exception Handling: Use try-catch blocks for operations that might predictably fail (e.g., external API calls).

Clear Error Explanations (AI Interaction): When encountering errors during development, clearly explain the error, propose potential solutions, and justify the chosen fix.

X. Testing

Use WP Test Framework: Utilize the WordPress automated testing framework (based on PHPUnit, using WP_UnitTestCase) for unit and integration tests where applicable.

Regular Testing: Perform tests (unit, integration, compatibility, performance) after significant code changes to ensure stability and prevent regressions.

XI. Documentation

Code Documentation (PHPDoc): Write clear, comprehensive PHPDoc blocks (following WordPress standards) for all classes, methods, functions, and hooks.

Inline Comments: Use inline comments (// or #) to explain non-obvious logic or complex sections of code.

Readme Files: Maintain an accurate and informative readme.txt file following WordPress standards. Consider a README.md for repository usage.

Changelog: Keep a detailed changelog documenting changes, fixes, and additions for each version.

Hook Documentation: Clearly document any custom actions and filters, including their purpose, parameters, and example usage.

XII. Cleanup

Uninstall Routine: Implement a thorough cleanup routine in uninstall.php that removes all plugin data (options, transients, custom tables, files) when the plugin is deleted from the WordPress site.